<Vertical id="wp_activities_part1" title="Whose Phenomenon? — Activities" launchable="true">

  <Markdown id="wp_doc_intro">
  **Whose Phenomenon? — activity bank.** `wp_session.olx` mounts each exercise below on the screen that needs it; nothing here is a screen. The transcript is defined once and reused on the launch screen, in the Notes tab's annotator, and on the print page. Every writing box shows its word count until its floor is met.
  </Markdown>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       THE TRANSCRIPT  (defined once; reused on screen 4, in Notes, in print)
       =================================================================== -->

  <Markdown id="wp_transcript_body">
## The phenomenon: how Ms. Boyd introduces it

*Room 14, fourth grade, Tuesday at 1:15: the first day of her unit, Investigating Matter. You were at the back of the room. These are the first four minutes, word for word.*

**[Ms. Boyd dims the lights and plays a short clip: a wide, snow-rimmed lake, small figures skating in the distance.]**

**Ms. Boyd:** This is Torch Lake, up in Michigan, where I grew up. Every winter the whole top froze so solid we'd skate on it, and my dad would drill a hole and go ice fishing. Here's what puzzled me as a kid: if the lake freezes over, how are the fish down underneath still alive and swimming? Turn and tell your partner what you think.

*[Students talk in pairs. Ms. Boyd circulates, then calls them back.]*

**Ms. Boyd:** Okay — who can tell me what they think is happening?

**Marcus:** My grandpa takes me ice fishing when we visit my cousins up north! The fish are fine because the ice is only on the top.

**Ms. Boyd:** Exactly, Marcus — the ice is only on top. Great. So why *only* on top?

**Aaliyah:** In the big freeze last year our pipes busted and water went all in the hallway. My mama said the ice broke the pipe.

**Ms. Boyd:** Ooh — we'll get to ice and pipes later. Right now let's stay with our lake. So, why does the ice float on top instead of sinking to the bottom?

**Bri:** Wait — if ice is just frozen water, why doesn't it sink? It's the same stuff.

**Ms. Boyd:** Good question, Bri — hold onto it. Here's an easier one first. Who can name the three states of matter for me?

**Darnell:** Solid, liquid, and gas!

**Ms. Boyd:** Yes! Solid, liquid, gas. *[She writes them on the board.]* And ice — is that a solid, a liquid, or a gas?

**Class:** Solid!

**Ms. Boyd:** Right. Now — what do you think would happen to a bottle of water if I left it in the freezer overnight; would it stay the same? … It would freeze into ice, of course. So, back to Bri's question: ice is lighter than water, which is why it floats and the fish are safe down below. We're going to spend the whole unit on the states of matter. Diego, can you read the three states off the board for us? … Diego? … Okay — Marcus, read them for us.

*[Diego looks down and doesn't answer.]*
  </Markdown>
  <!-- ===================================================================
       SCREEN 2 — THE WORD
       The look-fors are learned by matching them, not by reading a list.
       =================================================================== -->

    <!-- October 2026: the qualities were renamed at the instructor's request
         (observable, puzzling, engaging, relevant, open-ended) and a sixth,
         inquiry-based, was added. The original five, kept for reference:
           Observable: Students can see, hear, or picture it happening. Not an
             abstract topic like "states of matter."
           Puzzling: It doesn't match what students expect, so it creates a real
             need to know.
           Rich enough to anchor a unit: Explaining it takes the target science
             ideas, not a one-word answer.
           Connected to students' lives: It links to experiences and communities
             students actually have.
           Held open: The class keeps working on it; the teacher doesn't resolve
             it in the first five minutes.
         (ids were wp_lf_observable, wp_lf_puzzling, wp_lf_rich, wp_lf_connected,
         wp_lf_open, each with a _def partner.) -->
    <CapaProblem id="wp_lookfors_match_problem" title="The framework's six qualities of an anchoring phenomenon" showAnswer="never" grade="immediate">
      <MatchingGrader id="wp_lookfors_match_grader">
        <Markdown id="wp_lookfors_match_prompt">Ambitious Science Teaching names six qualities of a good anchoring phenomenon. Match each quality on the left to what the framework means by it on the right: click the dot beside a quality, then the dot beside its meaning. The count of correct matches updates as you go, so keep adjusting until all six are right. This is a way to learn the terms, not a test.

**Correct so far: {{@wp_lookfors_match_grader.message}}** (the same count appears under the exercise)</Markdown>
        <MatchingInput id="wp_lookfors_match">
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_observable">Observable</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_observable_def">Students can see, hear, or picture it happening. Not an abstract topic like "states of matter."</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_lf_puzzling">Puzzling</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_puzzling_def">It doesn't match what students expect, so it creates a real need to know.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_lf_engaging">Engaging</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_engaging_def">It catches students' attention and gets them talking, the way a vivid video, demonstration, or story does.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_lf_relevant">Relevant</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_relevant_def">It connects to experiences and communities students actually have.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_lf_openended">Open-ended</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_openended_def">It has no one-word answer, so the class keeps working on it instead of settling it in the first five minutes.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_lf_inquiry">Inquiry-based</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_lf_inquiry_def">Students can investigate it: make predictions, test their ideas, and gather evidence across the unit to explain it.</Markdown>
        </MatchingInput>
      </MatchingGrader>
    </CapaProblem>
  <!-- inline: which of these is a phenomenon. Learning, not assessment: try
       until it is right; the wrong-answer note says why without naming the
       answer, and the chat moves on once the distinction has landed. -->
  <CapaProblem id="wp_pk_topic_problem" title="Phenomenon or topic?" showAnswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="wp_pk_topic_grader">
      <Markdown id="wp_pk_topic_prompt">Which one of these is a phenomenon, not a topic? Choose one and press **Check**. If it's not right, you'll get a hint; try again.</Markdown>
      <ChoiceInput id="wp_pk_topic">
        <Distractor id="wp_pk_topic_photosynthesis" value="photosynthesis">Photosynthesis</Distractor>
        <Key id="wp_pk_topic_maple" value="maple_leaves">The maple tree by the bus loop dropped all its leaves in three days</Key>
        <Distractor id="wp_pk_topic_states" value="states_of_matter">The states of matter</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="wp_pk_topic_water_cycle" value="water_cycle">The water cycle</Distractor>
      </ChoiceInput>
    </KeyGrader>
    <Markdown id="wp_pk_topic_right" when="@wp_pk_topic_grader.correct === correctness.correct">
*Yes. A tree, a place, three days, and no obvious reason. Something happened, and it needs explaining. The other three are what the book is about.*
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_pk_topic_wrong_photosynthesis" when="@wp_pk_topic_grader.correct === correctness.incorrect &amp;&amp; @wp_pk_topic.value === 'photosynthesis'">
*Photosynthesis is the name for what leaves do, everywhere, always. Nothing happened. Which one of the four happened once, somewhere?*
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_pk_topic_wrong_states" when="@wp_pk_topic_grader.correct === correctness.incorrect &amp;&amp; @wp_pk_topic.value === 'states_of_matter'">
*That is the chapter title. It is where a unit ends up, not the puzzle that makes anyone need to get there. Which one of the four is a puzzle?*
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_pk_topic_wrong_water_cycle" when="@wp_pk_topic_grader.correct === correctness.incorrect &amp;&amp; @wp_pk_topic.value === 'water_cycle'">
*The water cycle is a diagram: arrows, true everywhere. A phenomenon is one event in one place that the diagram has to explain. Which one of the four is an event?*
    </Markdown>
  </CapaProblem>

