  <!-- ===================================================================
       SCREEN 3 / SCREEN 5 — THE SCIENCE TALK IN HER QUESTIONS
       =================================================================== -->

    <CapaProblem id="wp_discourse_match_problem" title="The framework's kinds of teacher question" showAnswer="never" grade="immediate">
      <MatchingGrader id="wp_discourse_match_grader">
        <Markdown id="wp_discourse_match_prompt">Six things a teacher can do with a question. Two are kinds of **opening** question; four are **moves after a child answers**. Match each term on the left to its description on the right: click the dot beside a term, then the dot beside its description. Several of these are near neighbors, so expect a few tries. The count updates as you go; keep adjusting until all six are right.

**Correct so far: {{@wp_discourse_match_grader.message}}** (the same count appears under the exercise)</Markdown>
        <MatchingInput id="wp_discourse_match">
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_open">Open, reasoning question</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_open_def">An opening question that asks for an idea, a prediction, or an explanation: "What do you think is happening?" "What would happen if…?"</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_dg_closed">Closed, recall question</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_closed_def">An opening question that asks for a fact or the one word the teacher has in mind: "What are the three states of matter?"</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_dg_funneling">Funneling</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_funneling_def">After a child answers: a chain of narrowing questions that leads the class, step by step, to the answer the teacher already had.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_dg_focusing">Focusing</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_focusing_def">After a child answers: the teacher takes that child's idea and asks the class to build on it, so the idea stays the child's.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_dg_pressing">Pressing for reasoning</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_pressing_def">After a child answers: "Why do you think that? What's your evidence? Does everyone agree?" The reason has to come from the students.</Markdown>

          <Markdown id="wp_dg_reveal">The reveal reflex</Markdown>
          <Markdown id="wp_dg_reveal_def">After the teacher asks a good question: she answers it herself, or settles the mystery early. The thinking the question started stops.</Markdown>
        </MatchingInput>
      </MatchingGrader>
    </CapaProblem>
    <!-- Column labels carry no commas (the grid parser splits on them); rows
         are quoted because several of her questions contain commas. -->
    <CapaProblem id="wp_questions_problem" title="Ms. Boyd's questions, in order" showAnswer="attempted">
      <TabularMCQGrader id="wp_questions_grader">
        <Markdown id="wp_questions_prompt">Here are Ms. Boyd's eight questions, in the order she asked them. For each one, choose what it did:

- **Opened thinking up**: it asked the children for an idea, a prediction, or a reason.
- **Asked for a word**: it asked them to recall a fact or say the word she had in mind.
- **She answered it herself**: a good question, but she gave the answer before the children could work on it.

Then press **Check**. You'll see which ones you sorted the way the framework would, and the conversation picks up from there.</Markdown>
        <TabularMCQ id="wp_questions">
cols: Opened thinking up, Asked for a word, She answered it herself
rows:
  - "If the lake freezes over, how are the fish underneath still alive?|q1[Opened thinking up]"
  - "Who can tell me what they think is happening?|q2[Opened thinking up]"
  - "Why only on top?|q3[Opened thinking up]"
  - "Why does the ice float instead of sinking?|q4[Opened thinking up]"
  - "Who can name the three states of matter?|q5[Asked for a word]"
  - "Is ice a solid, a liquid, or a gas?|q6[Asked for a word]"
  - "What would happen to a bottle of water left in the freezer overnight?|q7[She answered it herself]"
  - "Bri's own question: if ice is just frozen water, why doesn't it sink?|q8[She answered it herself]"
        </TabularMCQ>
      </TabularMCQGrader>
    </CapaProblem>

