    <!-- The student finds the moments in the transcript instead of being told
         them. Turns are chunks (hidden § markers); the keys are Ms. Boyd's three
         moves, since the message is sent by what the teacher does with what a
         child offers. Students' own turns are optional so choosing them costs
         nothing; the stage directions and her other turns are neutral. -->
    <SimpleTextSelection id="wp_equity_moments" mode="graded" title="Who belongs here?" separatorRegexp="§" separatorHidden="true">
Look at what Ms. Boyd does right after a child speaks. Click each of her lines whose response to that child tells the class whose experience counts as science in this room, then press Check. The feedback will tell you if you've missed one.
---
Ms. Boyd dims the lights and plays a short clip: a wide, snow-rimmed lake, small figures skating in the distance. §
Ms. Boyd: This is Torch Lake, up in Michigan, where I grew up. Every winter the whole top froze so solid we'd skate on it, and my dad would drill a hole and go ice fishing. Here's what puzzled me as a kid: if the lake freezes over, how are the fish down underneath still alive and swimming? Turn and tell your partner what you think. §
Students talk in pairs. Ms. Boyd circulates, then calls them back. §
Ms. Boyd: Okay. Who can tell me what they think is happening? §
{Marcus: My grandpa takes me ice fishing when we visit my cousins up north! The fish are fine because the ice is only on the top.} §
[Ms. Boyd: Exactly, Marcus. The ice is only on top. Great. So why only on top?|marcus] §
{Aaliyah: In the big freeze last year our pipes busted and water went all in the hallway. My mama said the ice broke the pipe.} §
[Ms. Boyd: Ooh. We'll get to ice and pipes later. Right now let's stay with our lake. So, why does the ice float on top instead of sinking to the bottom?|aaliyah] §
{Bri: Wait. If ice is just frozen water, why doesn't it sink? It's the same stuff.} §
{Ms. Boyd: Good question, Bri. Hold onto it. Here's an easier one first. Who can name the three states of matter for me?} §
Darnell: Solid, liquid, and gas! §
Ms. Boyd: Yes! Solid, liquid, gas. And ice, is that a solid, a liquid, or a gas? §
Class: Solid! §
[Ms. Boyd: Right. Now, what do you think would happen to a bottle of water if I left it in the freezer overnight; would it stay the same? It would freeze into ice, of course. So, back to Bri's question: ice is lighter than water, which is why it floats and the fish are safe down below. We're going to spend the whole unit on the states of matter. Diego, can you read the three states off the board for us? Diego? Okay, Marcus, read them for us.|diego] §
Diego looks down and doesn't answer.
---
all: All three. Marcus's story is taken up and praised, Aaliyah's is set aside, and Diego is passed over for Marcus. Read together, they say who already belongs here.
>1: You have some of them. There are three moves of Ms. Boyd's that carry the message; each is what she does with what a child offers, or doesn't.
: Look at what Ms. Boyd does with what each child offers: Marcus's story, Aaliyah's story, and Diego's silence.
---
marcus: Marcus's ice-fishing story matches her phenomenon, and it is rewarded on the spot: "Exactly. Great."
aaliyah: Aaliyah's burst pipe is the same science, from something the whole class lived through, and it is set aside as off-topic.
diego: Diego, an emergent multilingual student, is asked to read aloud, doesn't answer, and is passed over for Marcus. Nothing in the launch gave him a way in.
    </SimpleTextSelection>

