  <!-- ===================================================================
       SCREEN 11 — RELAUNCH  (branch on the chosen anchor via UseDynamic)
       =================================================================== -->

  <!-- inline: choose the anchor (drives the UseDynamic branch above;
       each Key value MUST equal a hidden branch block id) -->
  <ChoiceInput id="wp_anchor_choice">
    <Key id="wp_anchor_memphis_freeze" value="wp_branch_memphis_freeze">Anchor it in last year's Memphis freeze — the burst pipes and boil-water week the whole class lived through.</Key>
    <Key id="wp_anchor_own_phenomenon" value="wp_branch_own_phenomenon">Choose a different everyday phenomenon of my own that anchors the same idea.</Key>
    <Key id="wp_anchor_lake_reframed" value="wp_branch_lake_reframed">Keep the frozen lake, but reframe how I introduce it.</Key>
    <Key id="wp_anchor_far" value="wp_branch_far">Something far away, and I will say how a child who has never seen ice gets in.</Key>
  </ChoiceInput>

  <!-- The four branch guides live in Hidden; UseDynamic mounts only the chosen one. -->
  <Hidden>
    <Markdown id="wp_branch_memphis_freeze">
**Your anchor: the Memphis freeze.** Strong choice — it's an event the whole class lived through, which levels the entry point. To keep it *rigorous*, make sure explaining it still requires the target idea (water expanding as it freezes), not just a story. Push on: *what actually broke the pipe?* Invite students' own footage, family stories, and the dripping-faucet advice everyone heard — and hold the mystery open before naming "states of matter."
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_branch_own_phenomenon">
**Your anchor: your own everyday phenomenon.** Good — just hold it to the look-fors. Is it *observable*, *puzzling*, and does explaining it *require* the science target (water expanding when it freezes)? Ideas that work: a soda can that burst in the freezer, a sidewalk or water bottle cracked after a hard freeze, ice cubes that always float in a glass. Make sure it invites *many* students' experiences, not one group's.
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_branch_lake_reframed">
**Your anchor: the lake, reframed.** A fair choice — the science is genuinely rich. The work here is the *framing*: don't open from your own childhood as the default. Start from what students have seen water and ice do, elicit and hold their ideas, and take up the connections they offer (like Aaliyah's pipe) instead of redirecting them. Watch that you don't assume frozen-lake experience is universal.
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_branch_far">
**Your anchor: far away.** Nyota's choice, and a defensible one. The question you owe the room is the door: what does a child who has seen ice only in a freezer do in your first five minutes? Not "connect it to their lives." What do they do.
    </Markdown>
  </Hidden>
  <!-- inline: justify against the three criteria -->
  <Vertical id="wp_redesign_justify_input">
    <TextArea id="wp_redesign_justify" rows="6"
              placeholder="Rigorous because... Culturally relevant because... Accessible to every learner (including Diego) because..." />
    <Markdown id="wp_redesign_justify_count" when="wordcount(@wp_redesign_justify.value) &lt; 12">*{{wordcount(@wp_redesign_justify.value)}} of 12 words. Write at least 12 to continue.*</Markdown>
    <Markdown id="wp_redesign_justify_more" when="wordcount(@wp_redesign_justify.value) &gt;= 12 &amp;&amp; wordcount(@wp_redesign_justify.value) &lt; 50">*{{wordcount(@wp_redesign_justify.value)}} words. That's enough to move on; the strongest answers here usually run closer to 50.*</Markdown>
  </Vertical>

